<DocsLayout title="Benchmarks" description="Locked-test results on 9 core, 20 Hugging Face and 21 OpenML datasets against a plain scikit-learn reference, repeated-seed intervals, new task types, and the nine reliability checks, with the weaknesses stated." current="benchmarks">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Reference</p>
  <h1>Numbers you can<br /><em>reproduce.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">Every score is on the <strong>locked test rows</strong>, which are opened once. The reference is a plain scikit-learn <code>HistGradientBoosting</code> fitted on the same development rows. Lower is better for log loss and RMSE. The tables below include the cases where SCMLE is behind.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>How to read them.</strong> One split and one seed per dataset means differences in the fourth digit are within noise. Choosing the best of many trials carries a mild optimism bias, which is why the locked-test score is the number to trust. These are results on public tables, not a head-to-head against practitioners.</p></div>

  <h2 id="reliability-checks">Reliability checks</h2>
  <p>Accuracy tables say how good a model is; they do not say whether the package keeps its promises. <code>python -m benchmarks.reliability</code> runs nine checks on synthetic data with known ground truth and exits non-zero if any fails. The measured value is written next to the threshold.</p>
  <DocTable label="Reliability checks" head={["Check", "Claim", "Passes when"]} rows={reliability} />
  <p>The coverage and problem-type methods have their own measured gates, written to a certificate file and checked for staleness against the code. Two gates missed their pre-registered floors and are listed as descriptive: CQR conditional coverage in the highest-noise decile, and the slice flag's familywise false-flag rate. Gates that need outside data or tools have not run and are listed as not measured. These checks complement, and do not replace, the leakage test suite, the mutation gate and the operational rehearsal.</p>

  <h2 id="core">Nine core datasets</h2>
  <p>400 s budget, 2 workers, every optional extra installed.</p>
  <DocTable label="Core benchmark results" head={["dataset", "rows", "task", "metric", "selected", "cv", "test", "reference"]} rows={core} />
  <p><strong>Honest reading:</strong> ahead of the reference on 7 of 9. The two exceptions are <em>wine</em>, whose locked test set has only 36 rows so one confident mistake dominates log loss, and <em>kc1</em>, where the selected stack trails the reference by about 0.026 PR-AUC on a 15%-positive set with few positives in the test rows, so the score is noisy. Titanic's near-perfect score comes from leaked columns (<code>boat</code>, <code>body</code>): the leakage screen flags both (<code>boat</code> by skill, missingness and record purity; <code>body</code> by record purity only, because it is recorded for 9% of passengers, all of whom died), and <code>leakage_policy="block"</code> refuses to proceed.</p>

  <h2 id="more-datasets">More open datasets</h2>
  <p>20 tables from the Hugging Face Hub and 21 more from OpenML, with a 240 s budget and 2 workers.</p>
  <h3>Hugging Face Hub (inria-soda/tabular-benchmark and scikit-learn tables)</h3>
  <p>Ahead of the reference on <strong>16 of 20</strong>.</p>
  <DocTable label="Hugging Face benchmark results" head={["dataset", "rows", "metric", "selected", "test", "reference", "vs reference"]} rows={hf} />
  <h3>OpenML</h3>
  <p>Ahead of the reference on <strong>13 of 21</strong>.</p>
  <DocTable label="OpenML benchmark results" head={["dataset", "rows", "metric", "selected", "test", "reference", "vs reference"]} rows={openml} />

  <h3>Reading the results</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ties at the third significant digit (ailerons, elevators, spambase, cpu_act, pol) are labelled by the unrounded numbers; treat them as ties.</li>
    <li>Chronological electricity data trailed the reference by about 0.05 ROC-AUC while the time column was only used to order rows; the reference, like most practitioners, used it as a feature. With <code>time_as_feature=True</code> the gap is 0.001. Models that key on absolute time must extrapolate, so it stays opt-in.</li>
    <li>The clearest weaknesses are covertype and letter (large multiclass sets where the 240 s budget leaves little room to tune), and house_sales, where the long-tail target transform won cross-validation but lost on the locked rows.</li>
    <li>Wide tables (bioresponse, 419 features) exposed that the post-selection diagnostics were not bounded by the time budget; they now share a fraction of it.</li>
    <li>Scores come from one split and one seed per dataset, so differences in the fourth digit are within noise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="seeds">Repeated seeds with intervals</h2>
  <p><code>--seeds N</code> repeats each dataset with N seeds (each seed changes the split and the models) and reports the advantage over the reference with a 95% Student-t interval. A win needs the interval to exclude zero; otherwise the verdict is <code>tie</code>. Three seeds, 150 s budget, the four bundled datasets:</p>
  <DocTable label="Repeated-seed results" head={["dataset", "metric", "test (mean)", "reference (mean)", "advantage (95% CI)", "verdict"]} rows={seeds} />
  <p>With three seeds only one result is distinguishable from noise (diabetes progression, ahead). The single-seed "behind" label on wine is not supported once the spread across seeds is counted: its interval spans zero by a wide margin because one seed scored badly.</p>

  <h2 id="task-types">New task types on real datasets</h2>
  <p>Every task type added after the first release was run on public data (OpenML, R datasets, the Criteo uplift sample, MSLR-WEB10k, covertype) with a 300 s budget per case and one seed, against a simple strong baseline scored on the same locked rows.</p>
  <DocTable label="Task-type benchmark results" head={["dataset", "task", "metric", "framework", "baseline", "verdict", "selected"]} rows={tasks} />
  <p><strong>Reading it honestly:</strong> these are single-seed point estimates, not intervals, so small gaps are ties (wine-red 0.572 against 0.567; yeast 0.4175 against 0.4195; mslr 0.5575 against 0.5583). Clear wins: AirPassengers, nottem, wine-white, scene, PBC, the randomized uplift sets, and covertype under a 50 MB memory budget. Clear losses: UKgas (the seasonal-naive forecast is better than the framework's direct ridge on 108 points) and energy-efficiency (a per-target boosted regressor is slightly better). On the observational Lalonde data the uplift estimate rests on the no-unmeasured-confounding assumption and its Qini is close to zero, so it should not be read as a causal result. Covertype's baseline is a default boosted model on all development rows, which is weak, so its margin overstates the framework's advantage.</p>

  <h2>Reproducing the numbers</h2>
  <pre><code>{`python -m benchmarks.run_benchmarks --offline --output results.json
python -m benchmarks.run_benchmarks --suite huggingface
python -m benchmarks.run_benchmarks --suite extended
python -m benchmarks.check_regression results.json
python -m benchmarks.reliability`}</code></pre>
  <p>Every other page cites numbers from this one. How benchmark gates feed the review cadence is on <a href={`${base}/docs/operating-loop/`}>Operating loop</a>.</p>

  <p class="meta">Results as recorded for the 0.1 beta line</p>
</DocsLayout>
